WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:03.336
Three, Two, One, Zero.

00:00:05.463 --> 00:00:08.383
Let's begin with the simplest of space math.

00:00:09.467 --> 00:00:11.678
From 1960 to 2020,

00:00:12.095 --> 00:00:13.805
the number of satellites in orbit

00:00:13.805 --> 00:00:16.307
increased at a slow and steady pace.

00:00:17.642 --> 00:00:19.644
We went from zero satellites

00:00:19.644 --> 00:00:21.855
to about 2,500 satellites,

00:00:22.772 --> 00:00:24.983
zipping by over our heads.

00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:28.361
By the end of 2021, however,

00:00:28.653 --> 00:00:31.448
that number had doubled to 5,000.

00:00:32.449 --> 00:00:35.076
And this is not an anomaly.

00:00:35.076 --> 00:00:37.537
5,000 will soon turn to 10,000

00:00:37.662 --> 00:00:41.833
and then, deep breaths,
100,000 satellites.

00:00:42.125 --> 00:00:44.335
If companies like SpaceX, Amazon,

00:00:44.794 --> 00:00:47.464
and many others get their way,

00:00:47.464 --> 00:00:50.216
most of these satellites
will settle in low earth orbit,

00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:52.635
which is right here.

00:00:52.761 --> 00:00:54.846
It might seem like a roomy enough place,

00:00:54.846 --> 00:00:58.266
but it's already filled
with those existing satellites, leftover

00:00:58.266 --> 00:01:01.186
rocket bodies and debris from collisions

00:01:01.936 --> 00:01:05.482
in some countries
- Russia, we're looking at you

00:01:05.482 --> 00:01:07.901
- have only made matters worse

00:01:08.651 --> 00:01:10.987
by firing missiles into space

00:01:10.987 --> 00:01:12.655
and blowing things up.

00:01:12.655 --> 00:01:15.283
What we have here is a real problem,

00:01:15.992 --> 00:01:18.286
the kind of problem
that could ruin space now

00:01:18.703 --> 00:01:20.538
and for many years into the future.

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:23.500
If only someone,

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.086
somewhere was doing something about it.

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:45.522
Today

00:01:45.522 --> 00:01:47.982
I am going to go visit a company called

00:01:47.982 --> 00:01:50.193
LeoLabs, it's a startup

00:01:50.193 --> 00:01:52.445
based right here in Silicon Valley.

00:01:53.404 --> 00:01:56.449
You can think of them as a sci fi,

00:01:56.449 --> 00:01:58.535
super modern air traffic control system.

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:01.996
They've built some technology

00:02:01.996 --> 00:02:04.082
that tracks everything in space,

00:02:04.749 --> 00:02:06.751
rocket bodies that have gone up there,

00:02:07.085 --> 00:02:09.254
satellites, all the debris

00:02:09.254 --> 00:02:11.798
from things that have collided
and exploded.

00:02:12.757 --> 00:02:16.469
It's kind of an essential technology
that we're going to need

00:02:16.845 --> 00:02:18.888
in the coming years, as space

00:02:18.888 --> 00:02:21.182
fills with more and more stuff.

00:02:21.182 --> 00:02:23.351
And so I'm going to head into their office

00:02:23.977 --> 00:02:25.645
and try and figure out

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:27.856
how this technology works.

00:02:33.528 --> 00:02:33.945
The core of

00:02:33.945 --> 00:02:36.322
what we do is tracking all the satellites,

00:02:36.322 --> 00:02:39.993
all the pieces of debris
in low-Earth orbit and the reason we're in

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:43.705
business is space
is gone into space race 2.0.

00:02:43.955 --> 00:02:46.541
And so the numbers of satellites
are scaling up.

00:02:46.541 --> 00:02:48.877
The risk from debris is scaling up.

00:02:49.294 --> 00:02:51.337
None of the tracking systems
were built to scale.

00:02:51.754 --> 00:02:53.715
And the reason we started the company

00:02:53.715 --> 00:02:56.759
is because we knew how to build
scalable tracking systems.

00:02:56.759 --> 00:02:58.469
So we're in the process of doing that.

00:02:59.762 --> 00:03:00.263
Dan and his

00:03:00.263 --> 00:03:03.016
co-founders started LeoLabs in 2015,

00:03:03.433 --> 00:03:06.019
and they appeared
just in the nick of time.

00:03:07.228 --> 00:03:08.646
The success of Elon Musk's

00:03:08.646 --> 00:03:11.858
Space X triggered a revolution
in the space industry.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:15.069
Almost out of nowhere,
the world became awash

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:17.113
in rocket and satellite startups.

00:03:17.947 --> 00:03:19.866
More people than ever before

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:23.286
want to put more stuff than ever
before into orbit.

00:03:23.953 --> 00:03:25.538
The LeoLabs founders saw

00:03:25.538 --> 00:03:27.790
this frenzy of activity coming

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:30.877
and they bet that we would soon need
better systems

00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:34.214
for tracking things in space.

00:03:34.505 --> 00:03:35.965
You guys were ahead of the game

00:03:35.965 --> 00:03:39.552
in kind of predicting
where this economy was going.

00:03:39.802 --> 00:03:41.888
Yeah, that's
the whole reason we're in business.

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:44.349
The number of satellites has gone up
so dramatically.

00:03:44.557 --> 00:03:48.102
We focus on low-Earth orbit,
so kind of the closest 2,000

00:03:48.102 --> 00:03:50.355
kilometers to the surface of the earth.

00:03:50.355 --> 00:03:53.733
That's
where the revolution in space is happened.

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:55.652
It's where the business revolution is.

00:03:55.652 --> 00:03:57.612
Three years ago, there were 800

00:03:57.612 --> 00:03:59.656
functional satellites
in that part of space.

00:03:59.948 --> 00:04:01.908
Now there's close to 4,000.

00:04:01.908 --> 00:04:04.827
On top of that, there are about 15,000

00:04:04.827 --> 00:04:06.829
pieces of debris tracked today:

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:08.831
dead satellites, dead rocket

00:04:08.831 --> 00:04:10.875
bodies and fragments of it.

00:04:10.875 --> 00:04:13.586
And there's been weapons
tests, there's been collisions

00:04:14.337 --> 00:04:17.257
on top of those 15,000 pieces of debris,

00:04:17.548 --> 00:04:20.468
there's about 250,000 critical

00:04:20.468 --> 00:04:23.346
but untracked pieces of debris
up there today.

00:04:23.346 --> 00:04:27.058
So a big part of what we've set out to do
is track that small stuff

00:04:27.225 --> 00:04:29.143
and bring it into the safety picture.

00:04:34.524 --> 00:04:35.024
Today,

00:04:35.024 --> 00:04:37.777
LeoLabs has raised more than $100 million.

00:04:38.528 --> 00:04:41.030
A lot of that money is going toward
the construction

00:04:41.072 --> 00:04:43.658
of a network of radars
placed around the world.

00:04:44.701 --> 00:04:47.245
It's these radar systems
that look up at the sky

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:50.498
and spot and catalog objects
as they orbit.

00:04:51.916 --> 00:04:54.210
You need this global network of radars.

00:04:54.585 --> 00:04:56.337
If you've got one radar site,

00:04:56.337 --> 00:04:58.631
you can check in on a satellite
about twice a day.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.718
So every 12 hours
that's a long time to wait.

00:05:01.843 --> 00:05:03.970
Really, you need sites around the world.

00:05:04.178 --> 00:05:05.680
You know, we've got four sites today.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:08.766
We're going to have more than 20 sites
over the next few years.

00:05:08.933 --> 00:05:12.186
So today,
you guys are in like Midland, Texas, Costa

00:05:12.186 --> 00:05:14.188
Rica, Alaska and New Zealand.

00:05:14.439 --> 00:05:15.982
That's right. OK, yeah.

00:05:15.982 --> 00:05:18.901
And then we've got three more sites
under construction.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:23.197
A big part of LeoLabs mission is not just
tracking objects in space,

00:05:23.406 --> 00:05:26.909
but actually making sure that satellites
don't collide into each other.

00:05:27.285 --> 00:05:30.121
Satellite companies
like Space X and Planet Labs

00:05:30.663 --> 00:05:33.291
pay to find out where their satellites are

00:05:33.291 --> 00:05:35.335
and if they're about to hit something.

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.630
A lot
of the satellites have propulsion systems.

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:42.050
They can kind of move
to get out of the way of each other

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:44.218
or change their orbit a little bit.

00:05:44.218 --> 00:05:46.512
It's a service that you guys offer to help

00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:48.890
these companies
know how to maneuver the machines.

00:05:49.223 --> 00:05:51.392
Yeah,
we offer a collision avoidance service.

00:05:51.392 --> 00:05:53.353
It's a subscription service.

00:05:53.353 --> 00:05:56.105
We'll send you an alert
up to seven days in advance.

00:05:56.314 --> 00:05:58.483
If your satellite's
going to come dangerously close

00:05:58.483 --> 00:06:01.027
to a piece of debris or another satellite.

00:06:01.736 --> 00:06:04.030
And most satellites have

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:07.408
thruster on board
a chemical rocket or an electric thruster.

00:06:07.617 --> 00:06:10.995
Companies have been doing that
for decades, moving satellites around.

00:06:10.995 --> 00:06:14.290
But it's sort of like a harder
problem now.

00:06:14.791 --> 00:06:17.126
The risk of a collision
is a lot higher now

00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:19.295
just because we've installed

00:06:19.295 --> 00:06:21.547
so much more hardware into space.

00:06:22.423 --> 00:06:24.801
You have a big collision,
it creates a cloud of debris.

00:06:25.051 --> 00:06:28.554
And now all the other satellites
are flying through this whizzing

00:06:28.554 --> 00:06:30.598
mess of debris.

00:06:30.598 --> 00:06:34.435
The other element of it is the need
for tracking all the small debris

00:06:34.435 --> 00:06:37.772
as well as we add
all of the new satellite into space,

00:06:38.064 --> 00:06:40.775
the risks of a collision,
the likelihood of the collisions going up.

00:06:41.609 --> 00:06:42.819
Right now, we're putting out

00:06:42.819 --> 00:06:45.905
about 400 million collision alerts

00:06:45.905 --> 00:06:48.408
per month, in low-Earth orbit.

00:06:48.408 --> 00:06:51.160
So these are either
the first alert of collisions happened

00:06:51.411 --> 00:06:54.080
or follow ups
letting you You said 4... 400?

00:06:54.372 --> 00:06:55.415
400 million.

00:06:55.415 --> 00:06:57.375
What's that

00:06:57.375 --> 00:06:59.877
mean, you're sending out 400 million...

00:06:59.877 --> 00:07:00.253
emails?

00:07:00.253 --> 00:07:01.546
You can get them over email.

00:07:01.546 --> 00:07:03.172
That's kind of the legacy way of doing it.

00:07:03.172 --> 00:07:04.257
It's machine to machine.

00:07:04.257 --> 00:07:07.218
So we push out these alerts
through our API

00:07:07.635 --> 00:07:11.097
and then the satellite operators
these days their fleets are automated.

00:07:11.264 --> 00:07:14.642
So when we send them an alert,
they can decide to nudge

00:07:14.642 --> 00:07:15.685
their satellite again.

00:07:15.685 --> 00:07:17.770
Or to a different
time to avoid the collision.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:21.315
To really

00:07:21.315 --> 00:07:23.568
understand how the LeoLab Service works,

00:07:23.651 --> 00:07:26.279
it would be super helpful
to have an astronaut,

00:07:27.071 --> 00:07:28.656
someone who spent time

00:07:28.656 --> 00:07:30.700
aboard the International Space Station

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:34.245
dodging debris on a daily basis.

00:07:34.245 --> 00:07:36.247
And as luck would have it,

00:07:36.706 --> 00:07:38.916
I found just such a human.

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:40.126
I'm Ed Lu.

00:07:40.126 --> 00:07:41.836
I'm one of the co-founders of LeoLabs.

00:07:41.836 --> 00:07:43.796
And you are an astronaut.

00:07:43.796 --> 00:07:45.548
I am a former NASA astronaut.

00:07:45.548 --> 00:07:47.717
I flew three times in space, two times

00:07:47.717 --> 00:07:51.762
on the space shuttle
then I launched on a Soyuz mission

00:07:51.762 --> 00:07:54.515
to ISS immediately following

00:07:54.891 --> 00:07:56.976
the Columbia disaster.

00:07:57.727 --> 00:07:59.896
When you're on the ISS,

00:07:59.896 --> 00:08:02.857
you guys actually do get pelted
by debris, right?

00:08:02.982 --> 00:08:04.901
Yeah, things do hit the ISS,

00:08:04.901 --> 00:08:09.197
I remember looking out on the solar arrays
and you can see holes in them.

00:08:09.197 --> 00:08:12.658
In fact, I remember doing a spacewalk
coming down a railing, and there's a thing

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:15.119
it looks like a bullet hole
in one of the railings.

00:08:15.119 --> 00:08:16.787
So I literally hit
a railing - a steel railing.

00:08:17.914 --> 00:08:18.998
Was it

00:08:18.998 --> 00:08:23.711
experiences
like that that drew you to this

00:08:24.003 --> 00:08:27.465
start up or how did you end up
getting involved with LeoLabs?

00:08:27.465 --> 00:08:28.132
Absolutely.

00:08:28.132 --> 00:08:30.218
So at the time,

00:08:30.218 --> 00:08:33.346
the number one danger to crews on ISS

00:08:34.055 --> 00:08:36.015
was orbital debris.

00:08:36.015 --> 00:08:37.308
And to this day, it is

00:08:37.308 --> 00:08:39.727
the number one danger,
according to NASA's own figures.

00:08:40.102 --> 00:08:43.189
And the reason is because

00:08:43.189 --> 00:08:45.233
the vast majority of objects
that are large

00:08:45.233 --> 00:08:47.318
enough to go through the hull,
should they hit

00:08:47.485 --> 00:08:48.986
the ISS,

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:50.404
were not tracked

00:08:50.404 --> 00:08:52.490
or are not tracked by the US government.

00:08:53.115 --> 00:08:54.534
And so this company was created

00:08:54.534 --> 00:08:58.538
to track those- the vast
majority of objects

00:08:59.038 --> 00:09:01.415
smaller than our tracked

00:09:01.415 --> 00:09:03.000
by the Department of Defense.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:05.545
And because they're a danger
not only to the astronauts

00:09:05.545 --> 00:09:08.256
but to all of development
of low-Earth orbit.

00:09:11.342 --> 00:09:13.135
As both Dan and Ed mentioned,

00:09:13.135 --> 00:09:16.389
Russia made the space issue all too clear
last year.

00:09:17.098 --> 00:09:19.600
It fired a missile
at one of its own satellites

00:09:19.809 --> 00:09:22.687
because, well, because it could.

00:09:23.479 --> 00:09:25.189
And now everyone has been left

00:09:25.189 --> 00:09:27.858
to deal with the consequences.

00:09:27.858 --> 00:09:30.611
So what we're looking at here
is the Earth,

00:09:31.237 --> 00:09:33.614
and these are LeoLabs'
radars are tracking.

00:09:33.698 --> 00:09:36.784
The orbit of this satellite, Cosmos 1408,

00:09:37.201 --> 00:09:39.829
was roughly along this line
and when you break it

00:09:39.829 --> 00:09:42.957
up, everything else
kind of ends up following that path.

00:09:42.957 --> 00:09:45.960
That's why all this stuff
is kind of looking like this.

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:48.337
So these are all the objects
we're tracking

00:09:48.337 --> 00:09:49.630
whenever any one of these things

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:52.425
goes through one of these beams,
we we recalculate its orbit.

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:56.596
Each of these beams can track about 2000
objects per hour.

00:09:56.887 --> 00:09:59.265
You've got
the green are actual satellites.

00:09:59.265 --> 00:10:00.266
Yeah. Yep.

00:10:00.266 --> 00:10:02.435
The yellow are rocket bodies. Yep.

00:10:02.476 --> 00:10:04.312
And the red is debris. Yep.

00:10:04.312 --> 00:10:05.396
And then blue is unknown?

00:10:05.396 --> 00:10:06.272
Yeah.

00:10:06.272 --> 00:10:08.441
It's important that you put your radars

00:10:08.441 --> 00:10:10.985
all around the earth
because that's how you get the coverage.

00:10:11.235 --> 00:10:12.612
You want to get
to the southern hemisphere.

00:10:12.612 --> 00:10:14.947
And that's because, well, objects,
they spend half of their time

00:10:14.947 --> 00:10:17.199
in the Southern Hemisphere,
and historically,

00:10:17.199 --> 00:10:19.869
people have left their trash up there.

00:10:19.869 --> 00:10:21.579
So those are dead things.

00:10:21.579 --> 00:10:24.081
The largest of the current
constellations is StarLink.

00:10:24.415 --> 00:10:26.626
They are covering the earth
in these things.

00:10:26.626 --> 00:10:28.794
There's
more than 2000 of these things already,

00:10:28.794 --> 00:10:31.005
and they're launching them in groups of 60

00:10:31.422 --> 00:10:33.049
every couple of weeks.

00:10:33.049 --> 00:10:36.427
This is a big problem
for satellite operators when they launch.

00:10:36.636 --> 00:10:39.555
And one of these rocket spits out
30, 40, 50....

00:10:39.555 --> 00:10:41.807
100 satellites.
Which one is theirs, right?

00:10:42.975 --> 00:10:44.727
Because they're not all, right?

00:10:44.727 --> 00:10:47.813
So one of the services
that we've been providing

00:10:47.813 --> 00:10:51.609
commercially to companies is "hey
figure out which one is which." Like

00:10:51.609 --> 00:10:53.861
if you look at this particular picture,

00:10:53.861 --> 00:10:56.113
you'd be like, "Oh, we're already f'd.

00:10:56.197 --> 00:10:58.240
This place is jam packed." But these....

00:10:58.240 --> 00:10:59.742
This is very misleading. Yeah.

00:10:59.742 --> 00:11:01.702
So these are much larger.

00:11:01.702 --> 00:11:03.037
For the purposes of display.

00:11:03.037 --> 00:11:06.374
Again, something that's the size of,
you know, China, right?

00:11:06.374 --> 00:11:07.041
Yeah. Yeah.

00:11:07.041 --> 00:11:09.085
You know, the size of a satellite
is going to be really small.

00:11:09.210 --> 00:11:10.252
It'd be less than a pixel.

00:11:10.252 --> 00:11:11.045
You couldn't see it.

00:11:11.045 --> 00:11:14.173
OK, so keep in mind
that there's a lot of satellites up there.

00:11:14.173 --> 00:11:16.842
There are many, many thousands
of satellites, but they're not this big.

00:11:18.511 --> 00:11:20.638
While my space math is simple,

00:11:20.638 --> 00:11:22.348
this space math is hard.

00:11:23.474 --> 00:11:26.143
It takes brainiac people like Bonnie here

00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:28.479
to find out when satellites might collide.

00:11:30.272 --> 00:11:31.649
As an astro person,

00:11:31.649 --> 00:11:34.777
I get to work on algorithms,
and those algorithms are

00:11:34.777 --> 00:11:38.072
what's going to go into the,
you know, server.

00:11:38.072 --> 00:11:40.157
And it's going to produce
some sort of alerts

00:11:40.157 --> 00:11:42.493
in the alerts on the CDMs.

00:11:42.576 --> 00:11:45.496
So the conjunction data messages.

00:11:45.496 --> 00:11:46.747
The heart of the algorithm

00:11:46.747 --> 00:11:48.999
is math and physics.

00:11:48.999 --> 00:11:51.043
We have two satellite in orbit

00:11:51.043 --> 00:11:53.295
and we know the path.

00:11:53.295 --> 00:11:55.297
And when they come through our radar

00:11:55.631 --> 00:11:58.092
we get an approximation
for position and velocity.

00:11:58.718 --> 00:12:00.720
So we know how fast these guys are going

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:03.472
and you know where they're going to go.

00:12:03.472 --> 00:12:04.724
If we have two of them

00:12:04.724 --> 00:12:06.642
going on a collision course,

00:12:06.642 --> 00:12:09.103
then we run something that is called

00:12:09.103 --> 00:12:11.230
like the probability of collision,

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:13.274
which is going to tell us, "Hey,

00:12:13.274 --> 00:12:15.985
at what exact time
these guys are going to come close

00:12:16.193 --> 00:12:18.779
and how far off they're going to be?"

00:12:19.238 --> 00:12:22.324
One of the things that you can see,
like the work that I do right

00:12:22.324 --> 00:12:26.287
now, is going into the safety of flight
and looking for conjunctions.

00:12:26.287 --> 00:12:29.707
So if you are satellite operator,
you know the name of your satellites,

00:12:29.707 --> 00:12:32.209
so you can just type it here in object one

00:12:33.127 --> 00:12:36.505
and then if you suspect
that you might be colliding

00:12:36.505 --> 00:12:38.799
with like another satellite
that you already know.

00:12:38.799 --> 00:12:41.343
So for example,
I don't know one of the StarLinks

00:12:41.343 --> 00:12:44.638
from Space X
or one of the Doves from Planet

00:12:44.638 --> 00:12:47.933
or anything, you could just type
the other object here in search.

00:12:47.933 --> 00:12:51.937
Let's just say that you have no idea.

00:12:51.937 --> 00:12:54.356
And let's just say that you are me,

00:12:54.356 --> 00:12:56.066
a Colombian citizen

00:12:56.066 --> 00:12:59.570
that is trying to find out
what's going on with that one

00:12:59.570 --> 00:13:02.072
and only Colombian satellite
that we have in orbit.

00:13:02.281 --> 00:13:03.073
Let's look it out.

00:13:03.073 --> 00:13:06.494
So we go FACSAT, and there he is.

00:13:07.036 --> 00:13:10.039
We just want to see
what's coming in around FACSAT.

00:13:10.039 --> 00:13:11.248
So we do "search".

00:13:12.291 --> 00:13:12.625
and we

00:13:12.625 --> 00:13:15.878
have a potential collision with a StarLink
and you can see

00:13:15.878 --> 00:13:18.464
when that is going to happen
in the future.

00:13:18.464 --> 00:13:22.009
We can click on View
and it's going to take us to a page

00:13:22.426 --> 00:13:24.094
that is called an analysis report.

00:13:24.094 --> 00:13:26.222
It is going to show you the ground station

00:13:26.222 --> 00:13:30.142
so you can see what the path is that
the two satellites are actually following.

00:13:30.142 --> 00:13:33.604
So it tells you the exact time
when we think it's going to collide

00:13:34.146 --> 00:13:35.731
it gives you the miss distance.

00:13:35.731 --> 00:13:37.983
And so, you know, 20 kilometers in space

00:13:37.983 --> 00:13:40.319
is nothing like nothing at all.

00:13:40.736 --> 00:13:42.822
And so the actual collision

00:13:42.822 --> 00:13:45.074
probability is actually low.

00:13:45.074 --> 00:13:46.617
And so we know it's not like

00:13:46.617 --> 00:13:48.577
super high risk.

00:13:51.163 --> 00:13:51.705
Without this

00:13:51.705 --> 00:13:54.917
type of work,
space runs the risk of becoming unusable.

00:13:55.209 --> 00:13:57.545
Humans will have messed up low-Earth orbit

00:13:58.045 --> 00:14:01.507
in much the same way that
we messed up planet Earth.

00:14:04.343 --> 00:14:05.803
This debris is a big issue.

00:14:05.803 --> 00:14:06.929
There's this thing called

00:14:06.929 --> 00:14:09.098
the Kessler Syndrome,
I think is what they call it, right?

00:14:09.098 --> 00:14:11.058
Is that syndrome the right?

00:14:11.058 --> 00:14:11.475
yeah it is the Kessler Syndrome.

00:14:12.309 --> 00:14:13.435
So tell me how that works.

00:14:13.435 --> 00:14:17.606
I mean, it's this idea
of kind of cascading debris clouds, right?

00:14:17.606 --> 00:14:21.527
And the idea almost like a nuclear chain
reaction of one piece of debris

00:14:21.527 --> 00:14:23.988
hitting another piece of debris
and a cascade.

00:14:24.822 --> 00:14:26.073
I don't think we're close to

00:14:26.073 --> 00:14:28.367
an overall risk of Leo going into that.

00:14:29.243 --> 00:14:31.620
There are bad neighborhoods in space,
though.

00:14:32.079 --> 00:14:34.915
And so, in effect,
we're becoming a space realtor.

00:14:35.082 --> 00:14:35.749
We can tell you

00:14:35.749 --> 00:14:39.044
where the bad neighborhoods are and where
the good neighborhoods are between

00:14:39.044 --> 00:14:42.882
about 800 and 900 kilometers altitude
There's been some bad events.

00:14:42.882 --> 00:14:46.343
There's been weapons tests
where a satellite was blown up.

00:14:47.303 --> 00:14:50.264
There's been breakup events and the like.

00:14:50.264 --> 00:14:52.266
So there's just there's more debris there.

00:14:52.266 --> 00:14:54.560
So if you're going to put out
new satellites, you're going to launch

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:56.729
these satellites, don't put them there,
put them

00:14:56.729 --> 00:14:59.023
in other regions of space.

00:14:59.023 --> 00:15:00.190
The situation's manageable

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:02.526
right now,
but we do need to manage it better.

00:15:02.526 --> 00:15:04.361
There's satellites
going into all different orbits.

00:15:04.361 --> 00:15:06.614
You see them going
north, south and south, north

00:15:06.614 --> 00:15:09.158
and kind of east west
and everything in between.

00:15:09.742 --> 00:15:11.785
And it's just not organized.

00:15:12.286 --> 00:15:14.622
With a bit of organization it can....

00:15:15.164 --> 00:15:17.166
I think we can put a lot more satellites
into space.

00:15:18.208 --> 00:15:20.252
We've seen SpaceX

00:15:20.252 --> 00:15:22.254
with its StarLink communication system.

00:15:22.254 --> 00:15:24.757
They want to put up something
like on the order of like 10,000

00:15:24.757 --> 00:15:27.092
or more satellites, and they're not alone.

00:15:27.092 --> 00:15:30.262
There's multiple companies and countries
that want to put up

00:15:30.262 --> 00:15:34.183
tens of thousands of satellites for comms
networks and all sorts of things.

00:15:34.183 --> 00:15:37.603
And so if everybody gets their wishes
and there's

00:15:37.686 --> 00:15:41.065
100,000 satellites in low earth
orbit, like

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:45.402
is that actually feasible to manage,
if we have the right technology?

00:15:46.487 --> 00:15:48.530
I think we can totally manage it.

00:15:48.530 --> 00:15:49.949
That's the exciting part about this

00:15:49.949 --> 00:15:52.242
second space race is it's commercial.

00:15:52.242 --> 00:15:55.621
The leaders in it
are bringing new commercial services

00:15:55.621 --> 00:15:58.832
to market
and they're leveraging new technology.

00:15:58.832 --> 00:16:01.001
It's Moore's Law brought to space

00:16:01.001 --> 00:16:02.503
like you said,

00:16:03.128 --> 00:16:04.088
five, ten years

00:16:04.088 --> 00:16:07.299
ago, you'd spend ten years
designing and building a satellite.

00:16:07.299 --> 00:16:09.009
You run it for 20 years.

00:16:09.009 --> 00:16:12.054
And so at the end,
your technology would be 30 years old.

00:16:12.429 --> 00:16:15.349
And now companies are refreshing

00:16:15.349 --> 00:16:18.394
their satellite design every year
and launching new satellites every year.

00:16:18.394 --> 00:16:20.270
It's like keeping up
with the latest iPhone.

00:16:20.270 --> 00:16:23.107
I mean, you're getting new new chips,
new sensors, new cameras all the time.

00:16:23.315 --> 00:16:25.901
Exactly; new cameras, new computers,
new radios.

00:16:26.026 --> 00:16:27.111
It's awesome.

00:16:27.111 --> 00:16:28.904
To me, we're building like a computing

00:16:28.904 --> 00:16:30.906
shell around the earth.

00:16:30.906 --> 00:16:34.743
To sort of analyze it in ways
we never have before, whether

00:16:34.743 --> 00:16:38.455
it's photographing what's happening
with the rainforest and water

00:16:38.455 --> 00:16:42.501
and the movement of people and roadways
or the communication systems, it's

00:16:42.501 --> 00:16:44.795
kind of like this new Internet

00:16:44.795 --> 00:16:46.380
layer around us.

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:48.173
That's how I think about it.

00:16:48.173 --> 00:16:50.092
Yeah, I think about it the same way.

00:16:50.092 --> 00:16:53.470
It's in another way to look at is
business on

00:16:53.470 --> 00:16:55.514
Earth is just stepping into space

00:16:55.514 --> 00:16:57.683
in a big way for the first time.

00:16:57.683 --> 00:17:00.894
So now just normal day to day
stuff's getting routed through space,

00:17:00.894 --> 00:17:04.064
and that means it's communications
networks, cameras.

00:17:04.064 --> 00:17:05.399
Yeah, everything you said.

00:17:05.399 --> 00:17:07.026
This is the computer revolution

00:17:07.026 --> 00:17:10.112
in 1990, happening in space.

00:17:10.112 --> 00:17:10.696
Yeah.

00:17:10.696 --> 00:17:14.199
The monitoring and understanding of where
everything is and where it's going

00:17:14.199 --> 00:17:17.661
is going to be crucial to the development
of this economic sphere.

